1. What is DNS?
DNS (Domain Name System) is like the phonebook of the internet.
 We remember names (google.com, amazon.in) easily, but computers understand only IP addresses (like 142.250.183.110).
 DNS converts website names → IP addresses, so we can open websites without memorizing numbers.

2. How Does DNS Work? (Simple Explanation)
Whenever we type a website name in the browser:
1️⃣ Browser sends a request to DNS
2️⃣ DNS searches its database
3️⃣ DNS finds the IP address of the website
4️⃣ Browser connects to the server using that IP
5️⃣ Website opens on our screen

3. Real-Time Example
Imagine you want to call a friend named Rahul.
You don’t remember his number, so you check your phone contacts.
 You click "Rahul" → Mobile automatically dials his phone number.
Similarly:
You type www.youtube.com
DNS finds its IP address
Browser opens YouTube ✔
So DNS = the contact list of the internet.

4. What Are DNS Records?
DNS has different types of records stored inside it.
 Each record has a special job:

🔹 1. A Record (Address Record)
Maps a domain name → IPv4 address
Example: google.com → 142.250.183.110
Real Time Example:
 Typing facebook.com in browser → A record tells the browser the IP address of Facebook’s web server so the site opns.

🔹 2. AAAA Record (IPv6 Record)
Maps a domain name → IPv6 address
Same as A record but supports IPv6
Real Time Example:

🔹 3. CNAME Record (Canonical Name)
Used to create an alias for another domain name.
Always points to another domain, not an IP.
Example:
www.myshop.com → shop.mybusiness.com
Real Time Example:
 If a company rebrands the website, they can point the old domain to the new one using CNAME without breaking links.

🔹 4. MX Record (Mail Exchange)
Routes emails for the domain
Points to mail servers, not an IP
Example:
gmail.com mail server: https://lnkd.in/ekCywWXS
Real Time Example:
 When you send an email to xyz@company.com, MX records help deliver the email to the correct mail server.

🔹 5. NS Record (Name Server Record)
Indicates which DNS server is authoritative for a domain
Without NS records, domain won’t work.
Real Time Example:
example.com → ns1.godaddy.com, ns2.godaddy.com
These name servers store the DNS records of the domain.

🔹 6. TXT Record (Text Record)
Stores text information for verification and security
Mostly used for:
 ✔ SPF (email spam protection)
 ✔ DKIM (email signature & security)
 ✔ Domain verification for Google, AWS, Microsoft, etc.

🔹 7. SOA Record (Start of Authority)
First record in every DNS zone
Contains critical details:
Primary DNS server
Admin email
Serial number (version)
Refresh / Retry / Expire timing (for replication)

🔹 8. SRV Record (Service Record)
Defines the location of services
Specifies:
Service name
Protocol
Priority
Port
Target server

🔹 9. PTR Record (Pointer Record / Reverse DNS)
Converts IP address → domain name
Opposite of A record.
